Because let me tell you, there is!

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.500
Basically, what's going on here is that this year has seen some massive changes on the platform.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.200
Which merits a whole separate conversation unto itself.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.800
But the long and short of it is that my YouTube doesn't look like your YouTube anymore.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.700
You know what really proved this one to me?

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.800
Skibidi Toilet.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:26.400
Seriously.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.600
True story, I was recently talking to a big room of Google's higher-ups,

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.100
and I asked them if they'd ever seen a Skibidi Toilet video.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.200
You know, that insanely popular series of viral shorts that's gotten over 35 million subscribers,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.900
and 12 billion views on their platform in the last five months alone?

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.000
Not a single person in the room had ever heard of it.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.300
Regardless of whether you think Skibidi is pure gold or should be flushed down the toilet it came from,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.000
that right there, that is telling.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.600
And it's not just here on YouTube either.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.000
TikTok is really the one that started this whole trend.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.000
That's why you always hear people asking,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.500
Hey, what's on your TikTok?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.400
Because in a world where algorithms are selecting what content you see,

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.400
there's no guarantee that when I play this,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.600
That's suspicious.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.000
You know what I'm referring to.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.300
The good of this is that you're likely getting served content that's precisely targeted at your unique viewing habits.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.200
The bad is that fewer people are now watching the same things.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.900
And believe it or not, but watching the same things is a critical part of human survival.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.300
In short, if you've been feeling lonely, or anxious, or depressed, or potentially all three,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.200
it's because the world has become increasingly fragmented.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.400
And on-demand streaming entertainment is a perfect representative of why you're feeling that way.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.900
So let's talk about that, and also some ways that we can solve it.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.400
First, let's just get a sense of the scale that we're talking about here.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.200
Think of the biggest shows on television over the last decade.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.900
Two that stick out to me are Breaking Bad and Game of Thrones.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.900
Absolute darlings of the internet.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.700
There is no doubt that these were massive deals.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.900
Breaking Bad's finale reached 10.3 million people when it originally aired.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.000
Meanwhile, Game of Thrones captured 13.6 million,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.400
with another 5.7 million watching a rerun or streaming VOD within 24 hours.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.200
Those are incredible numbers for a modern show,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.700
and yet they're nothing compared to what TV shows saw pre-streaming.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.600
For example, let's take a look at Star Trek The Next Generation.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.500
When this show finished in 1994, how many people do you think watched the series finale?

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.900
More than 31 million.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.300
A show that, at the time, was considered by many to be niche nerd content,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.700
actually tapped into nearly three times the viewers of a modern program.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.000
And the numbers just get more insane as you look across more popular IP.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.300
52.5 million people watched Friends as it ended.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.100
Seinfeld got a whopping 76.3 million viewers.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:38.400
I kid you not, people were literally gathered at Times Square to watch the finale together.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.700
Cheers was sent off with 80.4 million viewers,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.700
and MASH ended with nearly 106 million people,

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.700
making it the single most-watched broadcast in American history

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.100
that wasn't a Super Bowl, a president resigning, or a moon landing.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.200
Sure, MrBeast can pull 100 million viewers no sweat,

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.500
but that's talking global numbers across the span of a couple days,

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.900
with ads that are randomly decided by an algorithm based on your tastes

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.300
and randomly placed throughout the video based on how you tend to watch videos.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.400
By contrast, these massive TV moments were people all at the same time,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.100
all in the U.S., all watching an event unfold together in real time.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.600
It was scheduled into their lives.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.700
Streets were empty.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.600
Everyone was fed the same commercials at the exact same time.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.600
And when it ended, everyone was released as one big unit.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.000
So afterwards, when I said,

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.200
The tribe has spoken,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.600
or,
Wazzup?

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:25.700
or,
Eat my shorts,

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:26.200
you got it.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.700
You knew the references I was citing, and you were able to respond back in kind.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.500
It was a shared cultural lingo that you could reasonably assume

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.900
most people you talked to had also been exposed to.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.800
But think about what that means in the age of on-demand video,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.600
in the age of YouTube, of TikTok, and Netflix.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.600
What can you rely on people to actually know?

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:43.900
That's just a theory?

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.300
Yeah, maybe, but for as big as our channels are, we've always been niche.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.700
Like, comment, and subscribe?

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.300
Sure, but it's also too broad.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:50.900
It's too vague.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.900
That's on everything from a beauty vlog to a locksmithing how-to.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.600
It says nothing about who you are,

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.700
who we are as consumers of content.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:00.200
And of course, this isn't only affecting TV.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.100
Avengers: Endgame was the last big cultural movie moment to happen in theaters.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.000
And now, just four or five years later,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.200
the pop culture encyclopedia has largely run dry.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.900
IP like Marvel and Star Wars were among the last holdouts of shared cultural touchstones.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.400
But because of how they've been strip-mined for content,

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.600
their importance and relevance have largely been on the downswing,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.000
with ever-decreasing box office returns.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.000
And what's risen up to replace them? Nothing.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.500
Well, nothing except the Taylor Swift Eras tour streaming in theaters.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.100
But again, why is that such a big cultural touchstone?

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:35.200
Well, because she's one of the only artists to have made it big before this on-demand explosion,

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.300
with albums dropping in 2006, 2008, 2010, and 2012.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.700
All before Spotify came in and solidified music as the on-demand entertainment source it is today.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.600
And as a result of getting a foothold early,

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.100
she's been able to maintain relevance through consistent releases ever since,

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.200
leading us to today,

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.300
where she's one of the few music acts that have been able to achieve such huge critical mass

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.000
and cross generational boundaries.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.500
But after TayTay, who's next?

00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:59.200
Who rises up?

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.600
What musical acts have had the same sort of cultural relevance?

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.100
Internationally, I'd say K-pop comes pretty close,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.500
but the audience is now too divided for any one artist to attain that much of your attention.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:12.900
And so I can't just turn to the person next to me and ask if they stan Kim Taehyung,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.200
or make a casual reference to color-changing soup,

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:17.400
and just expect them to know what I'm talking about.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.400
Now, on one hand, this rise of on-demand viewing is a good thing.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.000
Theoretically, you're being shown things that actually interest you.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.800
Videos, movies, games, and music that are all tailored to your specific individual wants and needs,

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:32.800
instead of what a corporate board of directors in Burbank, California deemed to be the next popular thing.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:38.000
But because we don't know what anyone else has seen, is listening to, is playing, quoting, or memeing,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.100
we lose the pop in pop culture.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:45.500
At the end of the day, you don't have popular culture if nothing is actually popular with everyone.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.900
But why does that matter?

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.300
Can't we all just live in our happy little bubbles and carry on?

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:53.300
Well, it turns out that at the end of the day, that bubble doesn't end up being very happy.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.200
The reason is that popular culture supplies us with a lot more than hummable tunes

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.400
and weird euphoria clips where everyone's crying.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.500
It supplies us with connection.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.600
One of the easiest ways to connect with someone that you don't know

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:06.600
is to find a piece of IP that you both know something about.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.100
Wait a minute, you also watched the finale of Seinfeld?

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.900
Well, hello!

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:12.100
Oh, you saw Infinity War?

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.700
Why didn't they just go for the head?

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:14.900
You listened to Taylor Swift too?

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:16.900
That's awesome! What era's your favorite?

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:19.600
But as pop culture becomes more individualized,

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:23.500
the group we feel that we can truly connect with becomes smaller and smaller.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:26.200
Does that sound like I'm overstating the importance of these connections?

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.600
Well, it turns out the fracturing of pop culture platforms

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:33.400
coincides with a huge rise in feelings of loneliness and isolation in our society.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.800
Especially here in the US, where we've already placed a high value on the idea of independence.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:42.000
A Gallup poll from 1980 showed that 20% of the population felt lonely.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.300
That same poll in 2020 reported that 36% of people,

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:51.400
more than 1 in 3, was grappling with some form of serious loneliness.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.400
But that was 2020, right? That was the pandemic.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.500
You'd think that coming back from the pandemic would have improved the situation.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.800
But polls in 2023, two years after lockdowns have ended in most places,

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.400
they show that loneliness continues to climb,

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:09.900
with some studies reporting 60% of respondents feeling isolated, lonely, or struggling to make personal connections.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.700
And these stats aren't just like, oh, that's really sad.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:17.300
It's an epidemic that disproportionately affects people under the age of 25.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.600
That's not to say older generations haven't dealt with mental health or loneliness.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:24.700
They do, frequently, but it often goes underdiagnosed in those populations.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:28.900
That said, the statistics overwhelmingly show that the vast majority of younger people

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.300
are dealing with loneliness on a regular basis.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:35.500
And they're, of course, also the people that have spent their lives in this fractured pop cultural world

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.300
with fewer anchor points that connect them to other people.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:40.200
Instead, the only things that unite us now,

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:43.900
the only things that are truly able to cut through the noise are the tragedies.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:49.000
In an absence of fun moments like a new Star Wars movie, or an Avengers film, or a new concert,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.200
bad news is the thing that rises to the top of every social media feed.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.200
Natural disasters, shootings, war, political upheaval.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.600
And while it isn't bad that people are more aware of those problems and injustices,

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:04.300
this constant cycle of never-ending bad news with absolutely no levity cutting through

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.500
has had a marked effect on the mental health of younger generations.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:12.100
But okay, I hear you saying that it's not fair to blame all our loneliness and isolation on these platforms.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:17.700
Like the fact that my TikTok doesn't serve me the same goalie cat clip yours does makes the world a sadder place.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.200
No, of course not. It's not only the platforms.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:25.400
But pop culture is part of a much bigger splintering that only looks worse the more you dig in.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.700
Pop culture obviously isn't the only way we relate to other people.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:32.400
There are lots of ways that people share interests and feel like we live in a society.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.700
And these ways are often listed out in various identity theories that have been made across history.

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:40.900
Sports, religion, your job, your ethnic background, maybe your political views.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.700
Any of these things might be enough to bring you together with someone else.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.100
The problem is that we're losing ground in these areas as well.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:47.900
All of them.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:49.900
For instance, looking at religious service attendance.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:54.100
It peaked in 1960 and it's been on the decline for the last 30 years.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:57.300
In fact, it dropped 10% since 2012 alone.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:00.000
What about our shared love of the good ol' U.S. of A?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.900
Patriotism in the U.S. has also plummeted across the last 10 years.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.200
Most significantly among younger people.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.700
On top of being the prime demographic to feel lonely and isolated,

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:13.000
young people on platforms like YouTube and TikTok are also more likely to be politically informed.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:15.900
And they've become cynical about their political identity.

