Or how about the biggest movie of the year, Barbie?

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:25.800
You know, the story about a person, a doll thing in a world where their role used to be clear-cut, but now she doesn't know what her place is anymore?

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.200
What her identity is?

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.000
Which has left her feeling, say it with me now, lonely and isolated.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.700
And she finds herself in the end by being a woman.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:39.000
And there's that big speech in there about how hard it is to be a mother and a woman and how there's so many expectations.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.300
Oh my gosh, it's almost like this whole movie is relatable.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:46.800
The stories that now cut through the noise don't shy away from this splintered feeling.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:48.900
Instead, they face those feelings head-on.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:55.700
Authenticity and honesty about the one universal that we all feel, loneliness, is actually what's helping to unite us.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:15:03.300
YouTube, which has been the world's most frantic viewing experience for the last three years, is just now fully finding itself swinging in the opposite direction.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.200
Simple conversations with Moist Critical about current events.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:10.500
Human experiences like eating with Eddie Burback and Ted Nivison at Margaritaville,

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:11.900
the lonely man's cuisine.

00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:15.400
Investing in human stories is feeling refreshing again.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:22.300
Even MrBeast has openly talked about pulling back from his ADHD-inducing editing style to a narrative format where he sounds more human.

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:27.100
And allows an actual story to unfold in his videos because that's what's keeping people engaged.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:31.100
Rather than just more and more suitcases of money opening up every ten seconds.

